          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p>Early Life of Archimedes<br>\nArchimedes was born around 287 BC in the city of Syracuse on the island of Sicily. He was the son of an astronomer named Phidias. From a young age, Archimedes showed a keen interest in mathematics, science, and engineering. He traveled to Egypt to study in Alexandria, which was a major center of learning at the time.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Contributions to Mathematics</strong><br>\nArchimedes made significant contributions to the field of mathematics. He is known for his work on the measurement of circles, accurately estimating the value of pi, and inventing a system for expressing large numbers. His studies laid the groundwork for calculus, which would not be fully developed until much later.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Inventions and Discoveries</strong><br>\nBeyond mathematics, Archimedes is famous for his inventions. He designed war machines for the defense of Syracuse, including the Claw of Archimedes, which was used to lift enemy ships out of the water. He also discovered the principle of buoyancy while taking a bath, which led to the famous exclamation \"Eureka!\"</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
