          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Archimedes' Screw</strong><br>\nOne of Archimedes' most famous inventions is the Archimedes' Screw, a device used for raising water for irrigation and for bailing ships. It consists of a large screw contained within a cylinder and as the screw is turned, water is drawn up its threads. This invention is still used in some countries today.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Archimedean Law of Lever</strong><br>\nArchimedes described the underlying principles of the lever, stating, \"Give me a place to stand on, and I will move the Earth.\" He demonstrated that placing a fulcrum at the appropriate point on a lever allows for moving heavy objects with a small force.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Geometry and Volumes</strong><br>\nArchimedes also excelled in geometry. He found the exact volume of objects with irregular shapes, such as spheres and cylinders, and he was the first to calculate the area under a parabola. This demonstrated an early form of integral calculus.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
