            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The Siege of Syracuse</strong><br>\nDuring the Second Punic War, Archimedes used his genius to invent weapons and strategies to protect Syracuse from the Romans. He created devices such as catapults and mirrors that were said to have set enemy ships on fire using the sun's reflection.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Death of Archimedes</strong><br>\nArchimedes died during the siege of Syracuse in 212 BC. According to historical accounts, he was killed by a Roman soldier despite orders that he should not be harmed. His last words were reportedly, \"Do not disturb my circles,\" as he was contemplating a mathematical diagram.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy of Archimedes</strong><br>\nThe legacy of Archimedes extends beyond his inventions and mathematical discoveries. His methods of problem-solving and his scientific inquiries laid the foundation for future generations of scientists and mathematicians. His work continues to influence these fields today.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
